sections.body:
Browsers enforce a **same-origin policy**: by default, JavaScript running on a page served from https://app.example.com cannot read the response of a fetch/XHR request it makes to a different origin, like https://api.othercompany.com, even though the request can often still be *sent* -- the browser blocks the *page's own script* from reading the response, as a protection against a malicious page silently exfiltrating data from a service the user happens to be logged into elsewhere. **CORS** (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is the mechanism a server uses to deliberately, explicitly opt back into allowing specific other origins to read its responses, via response headers like Access-Control-Allow-Origin.

A detail that trips people up constantly: CORS is a **browser-enforced** restriction on browser-based JavaScript specifically -- it has nothing to do with, and does nothing to stop, a server directly calling another server (via curl, another Node process's fetch, a mobile app's network layer), because there's no browser and no same-origin policy involved in that request at all. A missing or misconfigured CORS header only ever breaks or protects browser-based cross-origin calls; it is not a general-purpose access control mechanism for your API as a whole, and it should never be treated as your only line of defense against unauthorized access -- that's what authentication and authorization are for.

A common, genuinely risky shortcut, especially when a CORS error is blocking development and someone just wants it to go away, is setting Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * (allow literally any origin) rather than configuring the actual set of trusted origins that legitimately need access:

```javascript
const cors = require('cors');

// Risky: any website on the internet can now read this API's responses
// from a logged-in user's browser
app.use(cors());   // defaults to Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

// Better: only the specific origins that should actually be allowed
app.use(cors({
  origin: ['https://app.example.com', 'https://admin.example.com'],
}));
```

The wildcard configuration means that if a user is logged into your app in one browser tab, and separately visits a completely unrelated, malicious page in another tab, that malicious page's own JavaScript could make a request to your API (the browser will send the user's cookies along, if the API relies on cookie-based auth) and, thanks to the wildcard, be allowed to read the response -- exactly the kind of cross-origin data exfiltration the same-origin policy exists to prevent in the first place, deliberately reopened by an overly permissive CORS config. There's also a specific additional rule worth knowing: Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * cannot be combined with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true (needed for cookie-based auth to work cross-origin) -- browsers reject that specific combination outright, which is itself a signal that the wildcard and credentialed, authenticated cross-origin access are not meant to coexist.

Rate limiting caps how many requests a given client (typically identified by IP address, an API key, or an authenticated user ID) can make within a given time window, and it protects against several distinct problems at once. It defends against a deliberate **denial-of-service** attempt, where an attacker sends a flood of requests specifically to exhaust server capacity and degrade or deny service to legitimate users. It defends against **brute-force** attacks on something like a login endpoint, where an attacker (or a compromised, buggy client) tries a huge number of password guesses in rapid succession -- rate limiting the login route specifically makes that approach impractically slow, even if it doesn't make it impossible outright. And it defends against **runaway cost**, which matters especially for any API that itself calls a metered, pay-per-request downstream service (an SMS provider, an LLM API, a cloud storage service) -- a client (malicious or simply buggy, stuck in an unintended retry loop) hammering your API can translate directly into a large, unexpected bill from whatever paid service your API is itself calling on each request.

A basic fixed-window limiter tracks a counter per client key, incrementing it on each request and resetting it once the time window elapses:

```javascript
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const limiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,   // 15-minute window
  max: 100,                    // 100 requests per window per client
  standardHeaders: true,       // sends RateLimit-* headers so clients can see their own limits
  message: { error: 'Too many requests, please try again later' },
});

app.use('/api/', limiter);

// A stricter limiter specifically for a sensitive, brute-forceable route
const loginLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 5,                       // only 5 login attempts per 15 minutes per client
});

app.post('/login', loginLimiter, loginHandler);
```

A fixed window has a known, minor edge case worth being aware of rather than treating as a design flaw: a client could send its full quota right at the very end of one window and its full quota again right at the very start of the next, effectively getting close to double the intended rate over a short straddling interval. A token-bucket or sliding-window algorithm smooths this out more precisely, at the cost of somewhat more bookkeeping -- but for most APIs, a fixed window is a perfectly adequate, simple default, and reaching for a more sophisticated algorithm is an optimization to make once the simple version has proven genuinely insufficient, not a starting requirement.

examples.code:
const express = require('express');
const helmet = require('helmet');
const app = express();

app.use(helmet());

app.get('/health', (req, res) => res.json({ status: 'ok' }));

app.listen(3000);

// A response to GET /health now includes headers such as:
// X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
// X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
// Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15552000; includeSubDomains
// (plus several others helmet sets by default)

const cors = require('cors');

// Risky: ANY origin, including a malicious page in another browser tab,
// can read this API's responses from a logged-in user's browser
app.use(cors());

// Better: an explicit allowlist of the actual trusted frontend origins
const allowedOrigins = ['https://app.example.com', 'https://admin.example.com'];

app.use(cors({
  origin: (origin, callback) => {
    if (!origin || allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) {
      callback(null, true);
    } else {
      callback(new Error('Not allowed by CORS'));
    }
  },
  credentials: true,
}));

const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

// General API traffic: generous, since most routes aren't individually sensitive
const generalLimiter = rateLimit({ windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, max: 300 });

// Login: much stricter, since this route is a realistic brute-force target
const loginLimiter = rateLimit({ windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, max: 5 });

// An endpoint that calls a paid, metered third-party SMS API per request:
// stricter still, since each request has a real dollar cost attached
const smsLimiter = rateLimit({ windowMs: 60 * 60 * 1000, max: 10 });

app.use('/api/', generalLimiter);
app.post('/login', loginLimiter, loginHandler);
app.post('/send-sms', smsLimiter, sendSmsHandler);

